# README
# readrwf_grasp.f
## Conversion of RWFS ASCII Wave Functions to GRASP Binary Format

### Overview

The 'readrwf_grasp.f' utility converts the ASCII radial wave-function
files generated by the **RWFS** package into the binary wave-function
format required by the **GRASP** (General-purpose Relativistic Atomic
Structure Program) package.

The resulting binary orbital file can be used directly in subsequent
Multiconfiguration Dirac–Hartree–Fock (MCDHF), Relativistic Configuration
Interaction (RCI), and other GRASP calculations.

This utility is intended for users who generate orbitals with RWFS and
wish to use them as initial orbitals in GRASP calculations.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Requirements
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The program requires

- GNU Fortran ('gfortran')
- An ASCII wave-function file generated by RWFS (typically 'rwf.all')

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Compilation
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Compile the program using

gfortran -o readrwf readrwf_grasp.f

This command generates the executable

readrwf

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Input Wave-Function File
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The input file is the ASCII wave-function file produced by RWFS,

rwf.all

which contains all orbitals together with their radial grids and large
and small radial components in the ASCII format expected by
'readrwf_grasp.f'.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Running the Program
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The program is interactive. For automated execution, the required input
may be redirected from the command line.

For example,

./readrwf << EOF
2
rwf.all
carbon_atom_rwf.w
EOF

or

/path/to/readrwf << EOF
2
rwf.all
argon_rwf.w
EOF

where

| Input               | Description |
| :------------------ | :---------- |
| '2'                 | Selects conversion from the RWFS ASCII format 
|                     |   to the GRASP binary format. |
| 'rwf.all'           | Input ASCII wave-function file. |
| 'carbon_atom_rwf.w' | Output binary wave-function file compatible 
                         with GRASP. |

After successful execution, the output file (or any user-defined filename)
can be used directly as the orbital file in GRASP calculations.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Typical Workflow
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The recommended workflow is


RWFS calculation
        │
        ▼
     rwf.all
        │
        ▼
 readrwf_grasp.f
        │
        ▼
 GRASP binary orbital file (*.w)
        │
        ▼
 GRASP MCDHF / RCI calculations

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Output
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The program generates a binary GRASP wave-function file containing

- orbital labels,
- principal and relativistic quantum numbers,
- radial grid,
- large radial components,
- small radial components,

stored in the binary format required by the GRASP package.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Changing the Array Dimensions
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The maximum number of radial grid points is controlled by the parameters

fortran
      parameter (NNNP = 99590)
      parameter (NNN1 = 99600)

where

- 'NNNP' specifies the maximum number of radial grid points.
- 'NNN1' specifies the corresponding working-array dimension.

For correct operation, 'NNN1' should always exceed 'NNNP' by **10 grid
points**, i.e.,

NNN1 = NNNP + 10

If calculations require larger radial grids, both parameters may be
increased accordingly while preserving this difference. After modifying
the source file, recompile the program using

gfortran -o readrwf readrwf_grasp.f

The default values distributed with RWFS are sufficient for all supplied
examples and for most practical atomic-structure calculations.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Notes
----------------------------------------------------------------------

- This utility performs **only** the conversion of the RWFS ASCII
  wave-function file into the binary GRASP format; it does not modify the
  radial wave functions.

- The input file must follow the standard RWFS ASCII wave-function format.

- The generated binary file is fully compatible with the GRASP package and
  may be used wherever a GRASP orbital ('*.w') file is required.

- This utility is included for convenience to facilitate the direct use of
  RWFS-generated orbitals in GRASP calculations without any manual
  post-processing.
